            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="DUN05" sex="1">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>Andrew</forename>
                        <surname>Duncan</surname>
                        <addName/>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1000-01-01">
                        <placeName/>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1000-12-31"/>
                    <occupation>Attorney</occupation>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>See Ed Pope's notes:The second entry referred to William Nicholson's
                            attorney, Andrew Duncan of Holborn Court, Grays Inn, as explained, along
                            with Carlisle's presence, in Nicholson's letter to Godwin of the same
                            day (Bodleian Abinger c6 f134) brought to Godwin by Nicholson's son
                            William, though that wasn't recorded in the diary. He was probably the
                            Duncan of 1802 when Carlisle was present again, and perhaps the three
                            entries in 1805 and/or some of the later ones. He may have been the
                            Andrew Duncan gentleman of 70 Chancery Lane whose will was proved 1849
                            PCC. </p>
                    </note>
